coder › Implemented `toRoman`/`fromRoman` in `src/roman.ts` and added tests in `src/roman.test.ts`, on branch `work/roman` (commit `3bc8ef2`).

What changed:
- `src/roman.ts`: `toRoman` uses a greedy value table (M, CM, D, CD, C, XC, L, XL, X, IX, V, IV, I); rejects non-integers and anything outside 1–3999 with `RangeError`. `fromRoman` uppercases input, validates against `/^M{0,3}(CM|CD|D?C{0,3})(XC|XL|L?X{0,3})(IX|IV|V?I{0,3})$/` (rejects `IIII`, `VX`, `IC`, empty string, etc. with `SyntaxError` whose message contains the original text), then sums with subtractive handling.
- `src/roman.test.ts`: covers single symbols, all six subtractive forms, compound values (incl. 944, 1984, 3999), `toRoman` out-of-range/non-integer rejection, `fromRoman` case-insensitivity, invalid-numeral rejection with message check, and full round-trip `fromRoman(toRoman(n)) === n` for 1–3999.

Checks:
- `bun test`: `7 pass, 0 fail, 4080 expect() calls, Ran 7 tests across 1 file.`
- `tsc --noEmit --strict --target es2022 --lib es2022,dom src/roman.ts`: clean, exit 0. (Plain `tsc --noEmit --strict` without a target flags pre-existing-style issues: missing `bun:test` types for the test file and `Number.isInteger` needing es2015+ lib.)

Decisions not in the request: chose greedy table over digit-place lookup; `SyntaxError` message format is `Invalid Roman numeral: "<text>"`; `fromRoman` normalizes case via `toUpperCase()` and validates before parsing so only standard forms pass.

